<% content_for :title, "SpecGuard" %>

<%= render UI::PageComponent.new do %>
  <%# The subtitle names the product's purpose (Project Goals: suite intelligence for suites at
      20,000+ tests) and deliberately claims no capability. It used to read "Semantic test
      telemetry and duplicate prevention" — prevention was demoted by the owner on 2026-08-06 and
      the Goals article now says it "is explicitly not what the product is for". Which of the
      capabilities are actually available is answered by the two panels below, not here: a tagline
      is the wrong place to draw that line, because a reader cannot tell availability from tense. %>
  <%= render UI::PageHeaderComponent.new(
        title: "SpecGuard",
        subtitle: "Suite intelligence for 20,000-test suites — built for AI agents first."
      ) %>

  <div class="grid gap-4 lg:grid-cols-2">
    <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "Sign in") do %>
      <div class="space-y-4">
        <p class="text-sm text-app-content-secondary">
          Humans sign in with GitHub. CI and agents authenticate with a repository-scoped API key
          instead — you can mint one once you have registered a repository.
        </p>

        <% if github_oauth_configured? %>
          <%= button_to "Sign in with GitHub", github_auth_path,
                        class: UI::ButtonComponent.classes(variant: :primary) %>
        <% else %>
          <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, title: "GitHub OAuth is not configured") do %>
            Set <code>GITHUB_CLIENT_ID</code> and <code>GITHUB_CLIENT_SECRET</code> (or the
            <code>github.client_id</code> / <code>github.client_secret</code> credentials) and
            restart the app. See <code>config/initializers/omniauth.rb</code>.
          <% end %>
        <% end %>
      </div>
    <% end %>

    <%# == Why this is two panels rather than one ==================================================
        The single "What SpecGuard answers" panel this replaces led with
        ["Is this behaviour already tested?", "Prevention — /check-intent"]. Both halves of that row
        were wrong and they compounded: the route is not mounted (`config/routes.rb` carries exactly
        `get "repository"` and `post "ingest"` under api/v1), and prevention is no longer the
        product — a visitor who believed the panel went looking for a 404.

        The correction is NOT to swap one unbuilt promise for the five suite-intelligence
        deliverables, because four of them are equally unanswerable today: `Ingest::RunRecorder`
        writes `test_runs` and `test_run_shards` and nothing per test, so what a 20,000-example run
        persists is two integers. Per-test duration, duplicate clusters and everything built on
        them have no data behind them yet.

        So availability is the axis the page is split on, and the split is carried by the panel
        TITLES and by a warning at the top of the second one — never by a footnote or by a change of
        tense, which is what a hurried reader skips. The rule for anything added here: a row belongs
        in the first panel only if a signed-in user can go and read that figure off the dashboard or
        the API today.

        The telemetry half of the old panel survives in substance; it was directionally right.
     %>
    <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "What SpecGuard answers today") do %>
      <div class="space-y-3">
        <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
              ["How much of the suite can SpecGuard see?", "Annotated share, over the run's own total"],
              ["Where does the wall-clock go?", "Per run and per shard"],
              ["Is the suite growing, and how fast?", "Run over run, per branch"],
              ["Can an agent read that without a browser?", "GET /api/v1/repository"]
            ]) %>

        <p class="text-xs text-app-muted">
          Every figure is read off runs your CI has posted to <code>POST /api/v1/ingest</code>.
          SpecGuard does not run your tests, and ingestion never fails a build.
        </p>
      </div>
    <% end %>
  </div>

  <%= render UI::PanelComponent.new(title: "What SpecGuard is being built to answer") do %>
    <div class="space-y-4">
      <%# Leading with the warning rather than closing on it, because this panel's rows read exactly
          like the ones above and the whole point is that they are not the same kind of claim. %>
      <%= render UI::AlertComponent.new(tone: :warning, title: "Not available yet") do %>
        This is the product's direction, not its feature list. Ingestion currently records a run's
        totals and its shards and stores nothing about individual tests, so none of the answers
        below can be given today — and none of them is a surface you can call.
      <% end %>

      <%= render UI::DefListComponent.new(rows: [
            ["Which tests are slow, and which areas dominate the clock?", "Needs per-test duration"],
            ["Which groups of tests are potentially redundant?", "Needs suite-wide clustering"],
            ["What already exists for this area, and at which layers?", "Needs a row per test"],
            ["Which of those duplicates is safe to collapse?", "Needs both of the above"]
          ]) %>
    </div>
  <% end %>
<% end %>
